1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,119
In July 2022, BMW started charging rent on heated seats, not at a shop, not at a dealership,

2
00:00:08,119 --> 00:00:10,240
through the car's own screen.

3
00:00:10,240 --> 00:00:17,440
South Korea, the United Kingdom, Germany, South Africa, about 15 pounds a month or 150

4
00:00:17,440 --> 00:00:18,440
a year.

5
00:00:18,440 --> 00:00:20,839
Here is what made people furious.

6
00:00:20,839 --> 00:00:27,079
The heating wires were already inside the seat, copper, relays, switches, bolted in at

7
00:00:27,079 --> 00:00:28,239
the factory.

8
00:00:28,239 --> 00:00:32,880
You had paid for every gram of it, but a software flag said, not unless your car

9
00:00:32,880 --> 00:00:37,079
keeps billing, BMW did not forget to install a feature.

10
00:00:37,079 --> 00:00:40,359
They installed a hostage in your own front seat.

11
00:00:40,359 --> 00:00:43,119
Now, if you are rolling your eyes, fair.

12
00:00:43,119 --> 00:00:44,560
It is a seat warmer.

13
00:00:44,560 --> 00:00:45,560
Who cares?

14
00:00:45,560 --> 00:00:47,640
Except this was not a prank.

15
00:00:47,640 --> 00:00:51,000
It was a test, and somebody was taking notes.

16
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:56,280
Because one company had already tried this for years, and it did not start with a car.

17
00:00:56,280 --> 00:00:57,679
Meet HP.

18
00:00:57,679 --> 00:01:01,719
In 2016, HP pushed a quiet update to its printers.

19
00:01:01,719 --> 00:01:04,120
It was called Dynamic Security.

20
00:01:04,120 --> 00:01:06,079
Owners woke up to a dead machine.

21
00:01:06,079 --> 00:01:07,799
The ink cartridge was full.

22
00:01:07,799 --> 00:01:10,079
It just was not made by HP.

23
00:01:10,079 --> 00:01:13,000
No warning, no vote, just an update.

24
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:14,000
Overnight.

25
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:15,640
You did not buy a printer.

26
00:01:15,640 --> 00:01:18,200
You bought a printer that audits your ink.

27
00:01:18,200 --> 00:01:22,359
Italy's competition regulator find HP 10 million euros.

28
00:01:22,359 --> 00:01:23,920
That was 2020.

29
00:01:23,920 --> 00:01:28,239
Hetergroups in Belgium, Spain, Italy, and Portugal got a settlement.

30
00:01:28,239 --> 00:01:29,439
HP paid.

31
00:01:29,439 --> 00:01:31,239
The business model stayed.

32
00:01:31,239 --> 00:01:36,159
In 2024, HP's own CEO said the quiet part out loud.

33
00:01:36,159 --> 00:01:40,719
Every time a customer buys a printer, he said, it is an investment for HP.

34
00:01:40,719 --> 00:01:46,439
If that customer does not print enough, or does not use HP supplies, it is a bad investment.

35
00:01:46,439 --> 00:01:47,840
Read that slowly.

36
00:01:47,840 --> 00:01:49,560
You paid HP.

37
00:01:49,560 --> 00:01:52,680
And somehow, you became HP's investment.

38
00:01:52,680 --> 00:01:56,120
And the moment you buy cheaper ink, you become a bad one.

39
00:01:56,120 --> 00:01:58,159
A cold seat is an insult.

40
00:01:58,159 --> 00:02:00,400
A dead printer is an afternoon.

41
00:02:00,400 --> 00:02:04,400
But what happens when the locked machine is the one that feeds a family?

42
00:02:04,400 --> 00:02:08,159
A hog farmer in Nebraska stood in front of his state lawmakers.

43
00:02:08,159 --> 00:02:09,319
2017.

44
00:02:09,319 --> 00:02:11,120
His equipment had broken down.

45
00:02:11,120 --> 00:02:14,360
He told them there is no time to wait for a dealer.

46
00:02:14,360 --> 00:02:16,520
Farmers can often swap apart themselves.

47
00:02:16,520 --> 00:02:20,520
But the machine would not accept the new part until a dealer plugged in a laptop and

48
00:02:20,520 --> 00:02:21,520
approved it.

49
00:02:21,520 --> 00:02:22,520
The price?

50
00:02:22,520 --> 00:02:24,759
$230 to show up.

51
00:02:24,759 --> 00:02:28,520
Plus $130 an hour to plug in one cable.

52
00:02:28,520 --> 00:02:29,919
Whose tractor was it?

53
00:02:29,919 --> 00:02:32,800
So farmers did what cornered people always do.

54
00:02:32,800 --> 00:02:34,599
They went looking for outlaws.

55
00:02:34,599 --> 00:02:38,919
Hackers in Eastern Europe were already selling cracked tractor software.

56
00:02:38,919 --> 00:02:40,240
Forums in Ukraine.

57
00:02:40,240 --> 00:02:41,360
Pirate firmware.

58
00:02:41,360 --> 00:02:43,000
And the tractor wakes up.

59
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:45,080
Think about how backwards that is.

60
00:02:45,080 --> 00:02:49,960
An American farmer downloads cracked code so he can fix his own machine.

61
00:02:49,960 --> 00:02:52,520
Because the legal route is a dealer's laptop.

62
00:02:52,520 --> 00:02:57,599
John Deere's license agreement forbids nearly all repair backed by copyright law.

63
00:02:57,599 --> 00:03:01,560
The pirate respects the farmer's ownership more than the company does.

64
00:03:01,560 --> 00:03:03,719
So who signed off on all this?

65
00:03:03,719 --> 00:03:05,080
Not a villain in a cape.

66
00:03:05,080 --> 00:03:06,560
A spreadsheet.

67
00:03:06,560 --> 00:03:09,759
Wall Street prices every company with a multiplier.

68
00:03:09,759 --> 00:03:12,599
The tractor once and investors get nervous.

69
00:03:12,599 --> 00:03:14,080
What if a recession hits?

70
00:03:14,080 --> 00:03:15,879
What if nobody buys next year?

71
00:03:15,879 --> 00:03:20,280
So that company is worth maybe one or two times its yearly sales.

72
00:03:20,280 --> 00:03:21,599
Now watch the trick.

73
00:03:21,599 --> 00:03:22,800
Same tractor.

74
00:03:22,800 --> 00:03:24,039
Same factory.

75
00:03:24,039 --> 00:03:25,240
Same farmer.

76
00:03:25,240 --> 00:03:28,120
But now a monthly bill unlocks it.

77
00:03:28,120 --> 00:03:30,599
The accountants give the revenue a new name.

78
00:03:30,599 --> 00:03:31,599
Recurring.

79
00:03:31,599 --> 00:03:34,840
And investors start pricing it like software.

80
00:03:34,840 --> 00:03:39,039
Software gets six, seven, sometimes far more times revenue.

81
00:03:39,039 --> 00:03:40,520
Not for a better tractor.

82
00:03:40,520 --> 00:03:43,039
For a customer who cannot leave.

83
00:03:43,039 --> 00:03:45,800
Take a company with one billion in sales.

84
00:03:45,800 --> 00:03:48,120
Worth about one and a half billion.

85
00:03:48,120 --> 00:03:50,599
Convince investors it is a subscription business.

86
00:03:50,599 --> 00:03:52,360
At six and a half times.

87
00:03:52,360 --> 00:03:54,560
And it is worth nearly seven.

88
00:03:54,560 --> 00:03:55,560
Overnight.

89
00:03:55,560 --> 00:03:56,560
No new invention.

90
00:03:56,560 --> 00:03:57,919
No new feature.

91
00:03:57,919 --> 00:03:58,919
Just a paywall.

92
00:03:58,919 --> 00:04:00,879
On something you already own.

93
00:04:00,879 --> 00:04:02,680
And the CEO's stock options?

94
00:04:02,680 --> 00:04:04,400
Their value jumps.

95
00:04:04,400 --> 00:04:06,280
That is not simple greed.

96
00:04:06,280 --> 00:04:08,120
Greed would be easier to fight.

97
00:04:08,120 --> 00:04:12,520
It is a scoreboard that pays executives to take your ownership away.

98
00:04:12,520 --> 00:04:15,240
Because the idea is a hundred years old.

99
00:04:15,240 --> 00:04:16,240
Winter.

100
00:04:16,240 --> 00:04:17,240
1924.

101
00:04:17,240 --> 00:04:18,240
Geneva.

102
00:04:18,240 --> 00:04:21,600
A room full of men who sold the world its light.

103
00:04:21,600 --> 00:04:23,000
Osrom from Germany.

104
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:24,560
Phillips from Holland.

105
00:04:24,560 --> 00:04:26,480
General Electric from America.

106
00:04:26,480 --> 00:04:27,879
Rivals on paper.

107
00:04:27,879 --> 00:04:29,319
Partners in secret.

108
00:04:29,319 --> 00:04:30,879
They had one problem.

109
00:04:30,879 --> 00:04:33,199
Their light bulbs were too good.

110
00:04:33,199 --> 00:04:35,800
Some burned 2,500 hours.

111
00:04:35,800 --> 00:04:41,759
There is one in a California fire station that has glowed almost non-stop since 1901.

112
00:04:41,759 --> 00:04:42,759
Great product.

113
00:04:42,759 --> 00:04:44,399
Terrible business.

114
00:04:44,399 --> 00:04:47,199
Nobody comes back to buy a bulb that never dies.

115
00:04:47,199 --> 00:04:48,600
So they signed a pact.

116
00:04:48,600 --> 00:04:50,600
They called it Phoebus.

117
00:04:50,600 --> 00:04:54,199
Every bulb would be built to die after 1,000 hours.

118
00:04:54,199 --> 00:04:55,720
They tested the bulbs.

119
00:04:55,720 --> 00:04:58,920
Make a bulb that lasts too long and you were fined.

120
00:04:58,920 --> 00:05:00,800
Find for making a better product.

121
00:05:00,800 --> 00:05:04,560
A hundred years later, nobody needs a secret meeting in Geneva.

122
00:05:04,560 --> 00:05:05,680
No week filament.

123
00:05:05,680 --> 00:05:07,040
No cracked plastic.

124
00:05:07,040 --> 00:05:08,319
No evidence.

125
00:05:08,319 --> 00:05:12,519
Just one line of code and a server that says access revoked.

126
00:05:12,519 --> 00:05:13,519
Same incentive.

127
00:05:13,519 --> 00:05:14,800
Better paperwork.

128
00:05:14,800 --> 00:05:19,680
And if you think this only hurts people with BMWs, go to a small town in India.

129
00:05:19,680 --> 00:05:23,800
Take a delivery rider buying a smartphone on easy monthly payments.

130
00:05:23,800 --> 00:05:26,079
Phone sold this way can carry a lock.

131
00:05:26,079 --> 00:05:27,759
The lender controls it.

132
00:05:27,759 --> 00:05:29,480
He is late on one payment.

133
00:05:29,480 --> 00:05:30,480
A day.

134
00:05:30,480 --> 00:05:31,480
Maybe two.

135
00:05:31,480 --> 00:05:33,040
The screen goes dark.

136
00:05:33,040 --> 00:05:37,199
His map, his orders, his income behind a padlock.

137
00:05:37,199 --> 00:05:39,480
The loan app is not asking for the money.

138
00:05:39,480 --> 00:05:43,079
It is holding his job hostage until the money arrives.

139
00:05:43,079 --> 00:05:46,079
In America, regulators saw the same pattern.

140
00:05:46,079 --> 00:05:49,319
In June 2024, the government sued Adobe.

141
00:05:49,319 --> 00:05:52,240
It said customers were stuck in annual plans.

142
00:05:52,240 --> 00:05:54,800
Cancel early and pay half of what is left.

143
00:05:54,800 --> 00:05:56,600
So what happened to the seat?

144
00:05:56,600 --> 00:06:00,399
In September 2023, BMW quietly dropped it.

145
00:06:00,399 --> 00:06:01,600
No new conscience.

146
00:06:01,600 --> 00:06:04,480
Enough owners said no, loudly.

147
00:06:04,480 --> 00:06:05,800
Farmers got a law too.

148
00:06:05,800 --> 00:06:07,920
Colorado, 2023.

149
00:06:07,920 --> 00:06:10,720
The first date to write one for tractors.

150
00:06:10,720 --> 00:06:14,720
Minnesota and California passed their own for phones and laptops.

151
00:06:14,720 --> 00:06:17,920
It is the first time a few of them blinked.

152
00:06:17,920 --> 00:06:20,120
But look at what they were willing to try.

153
00:06:20,120 --> 00:06:21,120
A seat.

154
00:06:21,120 --> 00:06:22,120
A printer.

155
00:06:22,120 --> 00:06:23,120
A tractor.

156
00:06:23,120 --> 00:06:24,120
A phone.

157
00:06:24,120 --> 00:06:28,439
Whatever you own is one software update from becoming a lease.

158
00:06:28,439 --> 00:06:30,160
The seat is warm again.

159
00:06:30,160 --> 00:06:32,079
The question was never the heat.

160
00:06:32,079 --> 00:06:34,079
It was who's seat it is.

161
00:06:34,079 --> 00:06:39,519
Next time a screen asks for rent on something you own, remember who is holding the key.

162
00:06:39,519 --> 00:06:40,120
See you next time.
